{"name":"Sanskrit","hex":"#e69332","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Sanskrit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A classical Indo-European language of South Asia, which is the liturgical language of Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism, Jainism."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to Sanskrit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Sanskrit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sanskrit","blocks":[{"text":"Sanskrit is a classical language belonging to the Indo-Aryan branch of the Indo-European languages. It arose in South Asia after its predecessor languages had diffused there from the northwest in the late Bronze Age. Sanskrit is the sacred language of Hinduism, the language of classical Hindu philosophy, and of historical texts of Buddhism and Jainism. It was a link language in ancient and medieval South Asia, and upon transmission of Hindu and Buddhist culture to Southeast Asia, East Asia and Central Asia in the early medieval era, it became a language of religion and high culture, and of the political elites in some of these regions. As a result, Sanskrit had a lasting effect on the languages of South Asia, Southeast Asia and East Asia, especially in their formal and learned vocabularies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg/330px-BhagavadGita-19th-century-Illustrated-Sanskrit-Chapter_1.20.21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanskrit"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Sanskrit_College_1999_stamp_of_India.jpg/500px-Sanskrit_College_1999_stamp_of_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Sanskrit_College_1999_stamp_of_India.jpg/500px-Sanskrit_College_1999_stamp_of_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca